jitDotAssignT -- a JITTED `x.name = v` writes the field x, as the interpreted one does
(SEQ 297 R0; promoted from Clod's fixit jitDotAssign, which it retires).

Before the fix opAssign's jitting arm returned into jitEmitAssign before the accessor
hook, and the dot on the LEFT left gJitLastIsNode raised, so the store took the node
branch: the dot's product was stored into its own result node and the 1 was dropped.
jitted 0, interpreted 1, degrade 0. Now jitEmitDot records the node it seeded and the
value it seeded it with, and jitEmitAssign routes a store onto that node through
jitAccessorWriteRT, which calls the same setter opAssign's accessorWrite does.

Rows: JD-1 sets a flag (0 -> 1); JD-2 clears one `:.` set outside the jitted body (1 -> 0) -- each is the other's
non-zero sibling. JD-3 and JD-4 carry a VALUE (7, then 0) on lastIndent, the one write case
that takes more than a truth, read back inside the jitted body. JD-O is the interpreted
oracle, run after the jitted half (bear-trap 25).

Not covered, by design: `x.a = y.b` -- both dots publish into the one shared tempField, so
the left's product is lost; the emitter degrades it by name rather than store wrongly.

`:.` is set outside the jitted body on purpose: inside it, `jdB :. noPrinT` refuses as
`:. tempField` and the refusal then blocks every later store in the body (measured
2026-10-05) -- a separate jit gap, reported in the seal, not this fixture's question.
